    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "How Braces Can Correct Crooked Teeth: A Complete Guide"
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Home Blog 09 Feb, 2026",
      "links": [
        {
          "href": "https://drdantegonzales.com/index.php",
          "text": "Home"
        },
        {
          "href": "https://drdantegonzales.com/blog.php",
          "text": "Blog"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/blog/how-braces-can-correct-crooked-teeth-a-complete-guide/01.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "braces on crooked teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Crooked teeth are more common than most people think. Some are just slightly twisted, while others overlap or tilt because of crowded teeth, misaligned teeth, or poor jaw growth. Whatever the reason, the truth is simple: properly aligned teeth make eating, speaking, and smiling more efficient, more harmonious, and of course more spectacular. And yes, braces fix crooked teeth effectively when planned right."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "When we see crooked bottom teeth or gaps forming between front teeth, it’s not just a cosmetic issue. Over time, misalignment can lead to gum disease, jaw pain, excessive wear and chipping, and even tooth decay from the inability to clean the teeth well. So, correcting them early keeps your overall dental health stronger for life. That’s what all of the studies show, and what we have seen in our practice for over 30 years."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Why Teeth Become Crooked"
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/blog/how-braces-can-correct-crooked-teeth-a-complete-guide/02.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "crooked teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Primarily, it’s genetics. Other times, it’s habits or jaw growth abnormalities. Teeth drift as we age too, which surprises many adults. Every case of crooked teeth is slightly different."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Common reasons include:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "The teeth are too big for the jaws and crowd together in order to fit in the jaws"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Jaw alignment problems: when the upper and lower jaws don’t fit together properly."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Thumb sucking or mouth breathing during childhood."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Loss of primary teeth too early or too late."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Not enough space for permanent teeth, leading to crowded and crooked teeth."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Injury or trauma that moves the teeth out of the correct position."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Whatever the cause, modern orthodontic treatment gives several treatment options to fix crooked teeth, even in complex cases."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "How Braces Actually Work on Crooked Teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "It looks like simple brackets, wires, and rubber bands. But it’s an amazing science. Braces on crooked teeth use gentle, constant force to move teeth toward their desired position."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Here’s what happens behind the scenes:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Each metal bracket is bonded to the tooth’s surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "The archwire connects the brackets and guides movement."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Over time, pressure reshapes the bone around the roots, letting teeth straighten gradually."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "With regular orthodontist adjustments, we keep things moving in the right direction."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The body adapts naturally. Even severely crooked teeth can align beautifully over months of careful movement. Feels strange sometimes, but you get used to it."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Different Braces Options for Crooked Teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "When it comes to braces treatment, not every patient needs the same style. Some want invisible designs. Others prefer durable ones. Every orthodontist recommends based on the treatment plan, comfort, and how much movement is needed."
    },
    {
      "type": "th",
      "text": "Type of Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "th",
      "text": "Description"
    },
    {
      "type": "th",
      "text": "Best For"
    },
    {
      "type": "th",
      "text": "Visibility"
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Traditional metal braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Classic stainless steel brackets and wires."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Moderate to severe crooked teeth."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Noticeable but very effective."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Ceramic braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Tooth-colored and blend with enamel."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Those who want less visible options."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Less noticeable than metal."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Lingual braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Attached behind your teeth, hidden from view."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Professionals or adults concerned about looks."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Virtually invisible."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Clear aligners (Invisalign)"
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Removable trays that shift teeth slowly."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Mild to moderate misalignment."
    },
    {
      "type": "td",
      "text": "Almost invisible."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Each has its pros. Traditional braces and metal braces move teeth faster and handle complex cases, while clear aligners suit mild corrections and daily comfort. Lingual braces combine invisibility with strong force, though they need a short initial adjustment period."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Braces Fix Crooked Teeth in Different Ways"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "The technique differs depending on whether you have overlapping front teeth, crooked bottom teeth, or rotated molars. Every case starts with a proper treatment plan after an initial consultation and x-rays."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Here’s how we approach it:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "For crowded teeth, we expand or make space with palatal expanders if needed."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "For slightly crooked ones, small wire bends and regular adjustments do the trick."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "In complex cases, orthodontic treatment may use elastics or rubber bands to guide jaw alignment."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Once movement ends, a retainer locks everything into its desired position."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Even if your teeth are rotated or uneven, braces fix them step by step until we reach proper alignment."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Can Adults Get Braces for Crooked Teeth?"
    },
    {
      "type": "img",
      "src": "../assets/blog/how-braces-can-correct-crooked-teeth-a-complete-guide/03.jpeg",
      "alt": "",
      "title": null
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Adults Get Braces for Crooked Teeth"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Absolutely. Adult braces are common now. Orthodontic issues don’t stop after childhood, and we see many adults choosing teeth braces to fix long-ignored crooked teeth or spacing."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Adults usually prefer:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Ceramic braces for a discreet look at work."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Lingual braces or clear aligners for privacy."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Traditional metal braces for control in complex cases."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "It’s never too late. Teeth move at any age if the gums and bone are healthy. Orthodontic care just needs a little patience and steady cleaning habits."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "How Long Does Treatment Take"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Braces treatment for crooked teeth usually takes 12 to 24 months. Treatment takes longer when severe crooked teeth or bite issues are present. Age, bone density, and cooperation matter too."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Various factors that affect time include"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "How much movement your teeth need."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Type of braces you choose."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Consistency with regular adjustments and hygiene."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Following dietary care (avoiding sticky foods)."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Most patients start seeing visible changes within the first few months. That’s when self-confidence grows a little, and you begin to notice your smile shaping differently."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Other Orthodontic Tools That Support Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Some orthodontic treatments need extra tools to guide teeth straightening. It sounds technical, but it’s simple when explained."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Supporting tools include:"
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Palatal expanders for narrow arches."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Rubber bands to adjust bite balance."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Retainers for long-term hold after braces removal."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Small springs or elastics for closing minor gaps."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "These help braces work efficiently without causing discomfort. Sometimes a bit of jaw pain or pressure is normal, but it settles as the mouth adapts."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Importance of Oral Hygiene During Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Clean teeth move faster and stay healthier. Wearing braces means more corners where food particles hide. So daily care makes a big difference."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Brush after every meal with fluoride toothpaste."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Floss carefully between metal brackets using a threader."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Avoid sticky foods that cling to wires."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Schedule regular cleaning visits and orthodontist appointments for wire checks."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Good oral health protects from cavities, gum disease, and white spots. We always tell patients: small habits protect big results."
    },
    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "Fixing Crooked Teeth: Beyond Braces"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Sometimes orthodontic treatment combines other dental solutions after alignment ends. A few orthodontic patients choose finishing touches to polish the look."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Composite bonding smooths small chips or uneven edges."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Whitening brightens your straight teeth after braces come off."
    },
    {
      "type": "li",
      "text": "Minor reshaping ensures your dental alignment looks natural."
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "All of this is optional. But if your goal is a complete, beautiful smile, it’s worth asking during your review visits."
    },
